+# XPath Injection Vulnerability Fix
+
+## Vulnerability Identified
+
+**Type:** XPath Injection
+**Location:** `UserService.java:51`
+**Severity:** High
+
+## Vulnerability Description
+
+The original code was vulnerable to XPath injection attacks. The `username` parameter was directly concatenated into the XPath expression string:
+
+```java
+String expression = "//user[name='" + username + "']/role/text()";
+```
+
+This allowed attackers to inject malicious XPath expressions by crafting special usernames. For example:
+- `' or '1'='1` - Would match all users
+- `' or '1'='1' or name='` - Could extract unauthorized data
+- `'] | //user/credentials/password/text() | //*[@id='` - Could extract sensitive information like passwords
+
+## Fix Applied
+
+The vulnerability was fixed by using **parameterized XPath expressions** with `XPathVariableResolver`:
+
+```java
+// Use a variable placeholder instead of string concatenation
+String expression = "//user[name=$username]/role/text()";
+
+// Bind the variable safely through a resolver
+this.path.setXPathVariableResolver(new XPathVariableResolver() {
+    @Override
+    public Object resolveVariable(QName variableName) {
+        if (variableName.getLocalPart().equals("username")) {
+            return username;
+        }
+        return null;
+    }
+});
+```
+
+## Why This Fix Works
+
+1. **Separation of Code and Data**: The XPath expression structure is now separate from the user input
+2. **Type Safety**: The variable resolver handles the username as a data value, not as part of the expression syntax
+3. **Injection Prevention**: Special characters in the username are treated as literal values, not XPath syntax
+4. **Standards Compliant**: Uses the JAXP standard `XPathVariableResolver` interface
+
+## Verification
+
+The fix has been verified to:
+- ✅ Compile successfully
+- ✅ Maintain the same functional behavior
+- ✅ Prevent XPath injection attacks
+- ✅ Handle special characters in usernames safely
